  "articleBody": "After a hailstorm in Aquilla, you've likely had your roof inspected, and now you have a report in hand. Great! But sometimes these reports can feel a bit technical. Understanding what your hail impact assessment actually means is key to making informed decisions about your property. It's not just a document; it's your roadmap for what happens next with your roof.\n\nFirst off, your report should confirm if hail actually occurred at your property and, if possible, mention the approximate timing and direction of the storm. This helps validate the damage. Next, look for details about the components inspected. A thorough assessment covers not just shingles but also metal flashings, gutters, vents, siding, windows, and any exterior equipment. The report should clearly distinguish between different types of damage—is it hail bruising, fractures, punctures, or displacement? And just as important, it should differentiate these from wear, manufacturing defects, or damage from foot traffic or wind. This is a critical distinction that can impact your insurance claim and repair strategy. A good report will come with clear photos, labeled and mapped to specific locations on your roof, showing both damaged and (ideally) undamaged areas. If a ",
